(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-maintenance").sets.find(s => s.id === "1");
  const prose = [
  "Refactoring changes how code is organized while keeping the behavior that callers or users rely on. Extracting a duplicated calculation into one helper can be a refactoring if the same inputs still produce the same relevant effects. Adding a new discount rule is different because the expected result intentionally changes. Keep those purposes separate when practical: tests can protect existing behavior during restructuring, then new tests can describe the deliberate feature change.",
  "In this book, the difficulty of legacy code is tied to the absence of useful tests rather than simply its age. A program written last week can be risky to change if nobody can quickly detect a regression. Older code with good feedback may be easier to work on. The practical response is to establish tests around the behavior affected by a change, creating evidence that the edit did what was intended without silently breaking something else.",
  "A bug fix deliberately changes behavior that is considered wrong. If an expired coupon is accepted, the fix should make that case reject while leaving valid coupons usable. First state the intended result and reproduce the incorrect one. A test that fails before the repair and passes afterward helps connect the edit to the defect. Calling the work a fix does not make every behavioral difference acceptable; unrelated behavior still needs protection.",
  "An optimization aims to reduce a cost such as time or memory while preserving the results and effects that matter to callers. Replacing repeated searches with an index can be an optimization if answers remain equivalent. Measure the relevant workload to establish the benefit, and use correctness checks to protect behavior. A faster implementation that returns the wrong records has not met the goal. Also identify whether timing or resource limits themselves are part of the public contract.",
  "Before restructuring unfamiliar code, identify what people depend on it doing. A report's ordering, empty-input handling, or rounding may matter even if none is documented clearly. Examples and characterization tests can record that behavior before edits begin. This gives the team a way to distinguish an accidental change from an intended one. Understanding the current behavior does not require endorsing every quirk; it makes any decision to change a quirk explicit.",
  "Careful reading reduces mistakes, but it cannot reliably reveal every unintended effect after an edit. Feedback, such as focused tests, compares observed behavior with expectations while the change is still small enough to investigate. If a parser modification breaks empty fields, a relevant test can point to that immediately. The feedback must actually cover the risk being taken. A passing unrelated test or a successful compile does not establish that the affected behavior remained stable.",
  "Moving a repeated validation sequence into a helper is a refactoring when callers still observe the same accepted inputs, errors, and effects. The structure improves while the contract stays stable. Tests help check that claim, especially around edge cases. If the helper also changes which inputs are valid, the edit contains a behavioral change as well. Naming the two kinds of work separately makes review and diagnosis easier than treating every cleanup as automatically behavior preserving.",
  "A new feature intentionally changes what the system can do. Adding support for a second file format expands observable behavior, even if all old files continue working. A refactoring would reorganize the existing parser without that expansion. Both kinds of work can be valuable, but they need different expectations. Preserve the old cases with regression tests and describe the new cases explicitly, rather than relying on the word feature to justify unexplained differences.",
  "To justify an optimization, identify the cost being improved and the behavior being preserved. For example, caching a calculation might reduce repeated work while needing exactly the same answer for the same relevant inputs. A benchmark measures the speed benefit, and tests compare results. If input changes can make the cache stale, that is part of the correctness problem. Optimization is a constrained improvement, not permission to trade away required behavior without an explicit decision.",
  "A bug report such as \"checkout is wrong\" is too vague to guide a safe repair. Clarify the specific user-visible expectation, such as rejecting a purchase when stock is exhausted without charging the customer. Then reproduce the mismatch and write a test that distinguishes the intended result from the faulty one. This focuses the edit and review. Without a defined outcome, the team can change code substantially while disagreeing about whether the original problem was actually solved.",
  "The book's working definition emphasizes code without tests because tests supply quick evidence during change. A clean-looking new module can still leave developers guessing if edits alter important behavior. Conversely, an old module can become more manageable once focused tests surround the area being changed. The label points to a practical obstacle, not an insult about the original author. Improving feedback lets the team take smaller, more confident steps through the existing system.",
  "Place tests around the behavior your edit could disturb. If you change a fee calculation, include representative amounts, relevant boundaries, and any required side effects. Those tests can catch regressions while you reshape the implementation. Testing only a distant feature may miss the calculation entirely. The boundary should be broad enough to observe what matters, but focused enough that a failure gives useful information about the change rather than the entire application's environment.",
  "A test that always passes cannot protect behavior, even if it executes the relevant function. Ask what mistake would make its assertion fail. If a discount test only checks that the result is non-null, returning the full price might still pass. Checking the expected amount makes the test sensitive to the rule. This is why test quality depends on its setup and oracle as well as coverage: execution alone does not establish meaningful regression detection.",
  "A characterization test captures selected current behavior before restructuring code whose contract is unclear. You might record how an old formatter handles an empty list and a missing field. After the restructuring, the same cases can reveal unintended changes. The test does not prove the old behavior was correct; it establishes a baseline. If a baseline behavior must change, update that expectation deliberately as part of a separate, understood requirement or bug fix.",
  "Renaming an internal variable can improve readability without changing what users or callers observe. That makes it a design-oriented change rather than a new feature. Check whether the name is truly internal: reflection, serialization, public APIs, or external scripts may make an apparently local name observable. The classification depends on actual effects, not the size of the edit. Tests and dependency inspection help establish whether the rename preserves the relevant behavior.",
  "If a rule changes what customers see or receive, the edit changes behavior even when it is described as cleanup. Raising a fee threshold or changing which records qualify for a report needs an explicit expectation and suitable tests. It may be a feature or a correction depending on the agreed requirement. A pure refactoring keeps that rule stable. Separating intentional rule changes from structural edits makes it easier to understand and verify each purpose.",
  "A benchmark can show that a function finishes faster, but it may never check whether the answer is right. An implementation that skips half the records can look impressively quick. Pair performance measurements with correctness checks on relevant cases, and ensure the benchmark uses a representative workload. The two forms of evidence answer different questions. Faster execution is useful only when the behavior preserved by the optimization still meets the application's requirements.",
  "After one small edit, a new failure has a short list of plausible causes. After twenty unrelated edits, the same failure may require reconstructing a long chain of interactions. Small steps with frequent checks reduce that search space and make reversions easier. This does not mean every change is trivial; a large task can be divided into verified stages. Keeping each stage understandable helps the team learn where an assumption stopped holding.",
  "The function now charges three percent rather than two percent for the same amount. That changes its returned value, so it changes observable behavior. Even if the new rate is correct under a revised policy, the edit is not merely refactoring. Update the requirement and expected results accordingly, while checking other cases that should remain stable. The example shows why a tiny source change can have a meaningful business effect independent of its line count.",
  "A test protects only what it can observe and judge. If the important behavior includes sending a confirmation and saving a record, checking only the returned status can miss a regression in either effect. Choose a boundary that exposes the relevant result, perhaps through a return value or a recording collaborator. Avoid asserting unrelated implementation details merely because they are easy to inspect. The boundary should serve the behavior that must survive the planned change."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-maintenance/1");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-maintenance").sets.find(s => s.id === "2");
  const prose = [
  "Cover and Modify means establishing tests around the behavior you intend to change, then using them while editing. If you need to restructure a discount calculation, first capture representative current results. Run the tests as the structure changes so unexpected differences appear promptly. The tests do not remove the need to understand the code, but they replace some guesswork with repeatable evidence. Their value depends on protecting relevant behavior rather than simply increasing the number of tests.",
  "Fast tests let a developer check an edit while its purpose and details are still fresh. If a calculation test completes in a moment, it can run after each small change. A failure then points toward a recent, limited set of edits. When feedback takes hours, people tend to make more changes before seeing results, making diagnosis harder. Speed matters because it changes how frequently useful evidence can become part of the normal editing process.",
  "A short feedback loop connects an observed failure with the work that likely caused it. If tests passed, one method was changed, and a focused test now fails, the investigation has a clear starting point. If the next run happens after a day of unrelated edits, many more explanations are plausible. Short loops therefore save reasoning effort as well as elapsed time. The feedback must still be relevant and trustworthy; quick noise is not useful evidence.",
  "A broad system test may need databases, network services, accounts, and many components to work together. When it fails, the cause can lie in any of those places, including test setup. Such tests are valuable for integration behavior but can be slow and difficult to diagnose. Focused tests complement them by exercising a smaller unit under controlled conditions. Choose the level according to the behavior being checked instead of expecting one broad test to answer every question clearly.",
  "Tests record concrete examples of what the system does and what it should keep doing. When unfamiliar code is changed, those examples reduce uncertainty about whether important behavior survived. They do not eliminate all uncertainty because they cover selected cases under selected conditions. Read the tests to understand their scope and add cases relevant to the intended edit. Confidence should come from meaningful observations, not from treating a green indicator as a universal guarantee.",
  "A green result matters only if the test is capable of noticing the mistake you are concerned about. If a changed tax calculation is never called, the suite can remain green while every tax result is wrong. Even executing it is insufficient without a useful assertion. Confirm that the setup reaches the behavior and that the expected result distinguishes correct from faulty behavior. Then the passing test supplies evidence about that specific risk.",
  "Before modifying an existing rule, put a test around the result or effect you need to preserve. For a parser, that might mean checking how representative inputs become records. Once the test passes on the current code, small edits can be checked against that baseline. Sometimes dependencies must first be separated to make the test possible; keep those preliminary changes narrow and careful. Cover and Modify is a feedback strategy, not a claim that getting coverage is always effortless.",
  "Edit and Pray relies on making a change and hoping that careful thought plus informal checking will catch unintended effects. The problem is repeatability: the developer may forget a case or lack time to exercise it again. Automated regression tests preserve those checks and rerun them consistently. Manual exploration still has value, especially for discovering new behavior, but it is a weak substitute for a reliable check that should run after every relevant edit.",
  "When a test fails immediately after a small change, the developer can compare the intended effect with the actual difference while both are clear. This makes debugging more local. Long delays allow more edits and context switching, which expand the search and make assumptions harder to reconstruct. Tight feedback is therefore an aid to reasoning. It works best when tests are fast, deterministic, and focused enough that their failures say something concrete.",
  "A suite that takes a long time can become a check people postpone until the end of a task. That delay allows several mistakes to accumulate and makes failures harder to connect to individual edits. Keep a fast, relevant group of tests available for frequent use, with broader checks at appropriate stages. The answer is not to discard slow tests indiscriminately. It is to arrange feedback so developers can check local work often while retaining evidence about the integrated system.",
  "Calling a function in a test without inspecting a meaningful result may only establish that this execution did not crash. If the function returns an incorrect total, the test can still pass. Add an assertion tied to the intended behavior, such as the exact total or a required relationship. Also check important effects where appropriate. A useful test should explain what would count as failure, rather than merely provide another route through the code.",
  "Before repairing a bug, run the proposed regression test against the faulty implementation. If it fails for the expected reason, you have evidence that it can detect the problem. After the repair, a pass then shows a meaningful difference. A test that already passes may be exercising the wrong case or asserting too little. Investigate that before relying on it, rather than accepting a green result as proof that the bug has been captured.",
  "A passing test says that selected observations matched selected expectations in that execution. It does not prove every possible input, timing, or environment behaves correctly. This limited claim is still valuable when the cases are chosen for the risks of the change. Combine focused tests, broader checks, and code reasoning as appropriate. The goal is evidence strong enough for the decision, without pretending that any finite test suite is a complete mathematical proof of the program.",
  "A focused unit test controls a small subject and its relevant inputs, so a failure often points toward a local rule. A broad integration test may fail because of the subject, a collaborator, configuration, or setup. Both levels matter, but they provide different diagnostic information. Use focused tests for quick feedback on calculations and decisions, then integration tests to verify that the real parts cooperate. More scope is not automatically more useful for every debugging question.",
  "If an edit changes a branch that no test reaches, a passing suite provides no direct observation of that branch. A coverage report can help reveal the gap, but reaching the branch still requires a suitable assertion to detect wrong behavior. Add a case that exercises the changed condition and checks its consequences. This connects the test to the actual risk rather than relying on unrelated successes elsewhere in the application.",
  "Running a relevant test after each small edit makes feedback part of development instead of an inspection postponed until the end. The cycle can be simple: establish the baseline, make a change, run the check, and investigate any unexpected difference before adding more work. This keeps failures easier to explain. Choose a fast subset for local iteration, while still running required broader checks before delivery so local confidence is not mistaken for full integration coverage.",
  "A regression test preserves a lesson from a previous defect in executable form. If an empty import once crashed the application, keeping a test for that case prevents the team from relying on memory to check it again. The test should describe the intended outcome clearly enough that future changes can be evaluated against it. When requirements intentionally change, revise the expectation consciously. Otherwise the old failure can return quietly during unrelated work.",
  "Many edits between successful test runs create many candidate causes when a failure finally appears. The developer may need to separate interacting changes or reconstruct assumptions from hours earlier. Smaller verified steps reduce that burden and make it easier to identify which change introduced the difference. This is one reason fast tests improve productivity even when writing them takes time. They reduce the cost of discovering and locating mistakes during the work.",
  "The assertion checks that a member buying an item priced at 100 receives a total of 90. That expected amount makes the test sensitive to the discount rule: returning 100 would fail. Merely calling total_for would not provide the same feedback. The test covers this particular case, so other conditions such as nonmembers, zero prices, or rounding may need their own checks. Its strength comes from a concrete observation linked to an intended behavior.",
  "A protective test must change its result when the protected behavior changes incorrectly. If it remains green after a deliberate small mistake in the relevant logic, inspect whether the case reaches the code and whether the assertion checks the consequence. This is a practical way to question test strength. Do not equate failure sensitivity with asserting every internal detail; the test should detect meaningful regressions while allowing legitimate implementation changes that preserve the contract."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-maintenance/2");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-maintenance").sets.find(s => s.id === "3");
  const prose = [
  "A test needs an observable result so it can compare behavior with an expectation. If a method calculates a message and sends it directly to a device, the test may have no easy way to inspect the message. A recording substitute for the device can capture it for an assertion. That is sensing: making the relevant effect visible. The test should observe something that represents the behavior being protected rather than inspect incidental details with no contractual meaning.",
  "A unit may depend on a database, network service, or framework that is awkward to start in a focused test. Breaking that dependency lets the test exercise the unit without requiring the whole environment. For example, a supplied repository can return known records instead of contacting a live database. This separation makes conditions easier to control. It does not establish that the real integration works, so retain appropriate checks for that boundary as a separate concern.",
  "If creating an object immediately opens a network connection, a test cannot even obtain the subject without a working server and suitable credentials. That setup can be slow, unreliable, or inappropriate for the behavior being tested. Supplying the collaborator or moving connection work out of construction can make the object reachable in a test. Preserve the production behavior deliberately while introducing that flexibility, rather than allowing testability work to become an unreviewed functional change.",
  "A fake collaborator can record what the subject asks it to do. For example, a fake message sender can save the recipient and text instead of delivering an email. The test can then assert that the right message would have been requested. This provides sensing and can also avoid the real delivery dependency. The fake should model the relevant contract accurately enough for the case, while separate integration checks verify the actual sender's behavior.",
  "A focused test should not need the entire application environment when its question concerns a local decision. Replacing a remote lookup with a controllable collaborator removes network delays and unrelated failures from that test. The result is faster feedback with fewer possible causes when it fails. This is a deliberate test boundary, not a claim that external behavior no longer matters. Test the real connection separately where that interaction is the subject.",
  "load_user receives client rather than constructing a fixed network client internally. The caller can therefore supply a real client in production or a fake in a test. The function still makes the same get call, but the supplied object controls what that call does. This is the separation opportunity in the example. A test can also have the fake record the requested path, allowing it to verify that the function builds the correct user URL.",
  "A sensing problem occurs when the behavior happens but the test cannot inspect its relevant result. A report generator may write directly to a printer and return nothing useful. Replacing the printer with a recording object can expose the requested output without changing the report calculation. Distinguish this from merely making the code run. A test can execute successfully yet still lack the observation needed to decide whether the behavior was correct.",
  "A separation problem occurs when the subject cannot be exercised without dragging in an inconvenient collaborator. A fee calculation that constructs a live account service may require network setup even for a simple arithmetic case. Introducing a controlled way to supply account information can remove that obstacle. The purpose is to isolate the relevant behavior, not to replace every dependency automatically. Choose a boundary that makes the test meaningful and leaves real integration responsibilities explicit.",
  "Code that reads the real clock can behave differently depending on when the test runs. An injected clock lets the test choose a specific instant, such as just before an expiration boundary, without waiting or changing system time. That separates the rule from an external source of variability. The test still needs an assertion about the result at that instant. Controlling time establishes the conditions; it does not by itself judge the behavior.",
  "If a calculation's result is buried inside a side effect, exposing an appropriate return value can give the test something to compare. For example, separating invoice calculation from printing allows a test to inspect the calculated invoice directly. This creates a sensing point. Choose the interface so it expresses meaningful behavior rather than exposing arbitrary internals solely for tests. Keep any behavior change from the restructuring understood and protected as the boundary is introduced.",
  "A test double should implement the part of a collaborator's contract needed for the test, with enough fidelity to make the result meaningful. A fake repository for one lookup need not reproduce a complete database engine. Making it too elaborate adds another system that can contain bugs and obscure the case. Making it unrealistically permissive can also mislead. Keep its behavior simple, explicit, and tied to the inputs and effects the test is examining.",
  "A parameter can let a caller choose the collaborator used by a function. Production passes the real sender, while a test passes a recording sender. The subject's call to send stays unchanged, but the selected implementation changes its behavior. That is a possible seam. It is useful only if the function actually uses the supplied collaborator and the test can choose it; an unused parameter or a hard-coded replacement inside the function would defeat the purpose.",
  "A constructor that contacts a server can fail before the test reaches the method it intended to examine. The resulting failure says little about that method's logic. Separate resource acquisition from object setup or allow a collaborator to be supplied so the focused test can create the subject under controlled conditions. Keep tests for the real resource path where appropriate. The immediate goal is to reach the behavior being changed without requiring unrelated infrastructure to cooperate.",
  "A fake dependency may make the code easy to run, but the test still needs to see what happened. If a method returns nothing and the fake records nothing, a run without an exception may be the only available observation. Add a meaningful sensing point, such as a recorded request or returned result, and compare it with the expectation. Separation controls the environment; sensing supplies the evidence. Solving one does not automatically solve the other.",
  "A checkout test might be able to inspect its returned receipt yet still charge a real payment service every time it runs. It has sensing, but poor separation from a dangerous and inconvenient dependency. Supply a controllable payment collaborator so the test can exercise the checkout rule without a live charge. Then verify the requested amount and resulting receipt. Real payment integration belongs in an appropriately controlled test environment, not in every focused unit test.",
  "A recording fake turns a call that would otherwise disappear into observable test data. A fake notifier might store each notification's recipient and message in a list. After the subject runs, the test checks that list for the expected request. This verifies the subject's interaction without requiring actual delivery. Avoid asserting incidental call ordering unless order matters to the behavior, because overly detailed interaction checks can make harmless refactoring unnecessarily difficult.",
  "If a business rule fetches records through a supplied repository, a test can return a small known dataset without starting a database. That makes cases such as an absent record or a particular account status easy to construct. The rule can then be checked under controlled conditions. The repository interface should describe the operations the rule needs, and its real implementation still needs integration tests. Replacing the database in one test does not validate the database adapter itself.",
  "An assertion should examine the consequence the test is intended to protect. If the subject is responsible for choosing a discount, check the resulting amount rather than a private variable name or an unrelated log message. A recording collaborator can be appropriate when the required behavior is an outgoing request. The choice of observation determines what failures the test can detect. Easy access to an internal detail is not by itself a reason to make that detail part of the test contract.",
  "External services, clocks, and shared databases can vary independently of the code being edited. Controlling or separating those dependencies makes a focused test faster and more repeatable, so a failure is easier to connect to the subject's behavior. This improves the feedback loop. It also narrows what the test establishes: the controlled case does not cover every real-environment interaction. Combine local confidence with suitable integration evidence rather than asking either test level to do every job.",
  "Sensing asks whether the test can observe the relevant effect; separation asks whether it can run the subject without troublesome dependencies. A recording fake can solve both, but the questions remain distinct. A fake clock controls time without necessarily exposing a result, while a visible receipt does not prevent a real payment call. Identify which obstacle is present before changing the design. That keeps the dependency-breaking work focused on making a useful test possible."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-maintenance/3");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-maintenance").sets.find(s => s.id === "4");
  const prose = [
  "A seam is an existing point of variation where behavior can be selected without editing the code at the point being exercised. A method that calls an injected sender can use a real sender or a fake while that call remains unchanged. The selection happens elsewhere. This perspective helps locate testing opportunities in existing code: look for ways to replace a collaborator or binding rather than immediately rewriting the logic you need to protect.",
  "A seam needs some place where the alternative behavior is chosen. If a service uses a supplied repository, the constructor or call that supplies it is the enabling point. Production chooses the real repository, and a test chooses a fake. Merely imagining a replaceable collaborator is insufficient if nothing allows that choice to reach the code. Identifying the enabling point makes the test setup concrete and shows how the production and test paths are selected.",
  "Passing a collaborator into a constructor lets the object retain and use the supplied implementation. A test can construct the subject with a fake sender, while production supplies the real sender. Calls through that collaborator then behave differently without editing each call site inside the subject. This is an object seam when object substitution provides the variation. The constructor must actually honor the supplied object rather than create and use a hard-coded replacement internally.",
  "A test can use a seam to choose a dependency with predictable behavior. A fake clock might always return the instant needed to test expiration, and a fake repository might return a known record. The subject still executes its own logic. That distinction matters: replacing the behavior you are trying to test would make the result uninformative. Substitute the collaborator needed to control conditions or observe effects, while keeping the target behavior under examination.",
  "A seam is useful when it removes a concrete obstacle to testing relevant behavior. Allowing a database lookup to be replaced can make an eligibility rule easy to test with a few known records. Adding an interface that never affects the test setup provides little value by itself. Judge the seam by the control and observations it enables. The aim is a reliable test around the planned change, not a larger number of abstractions in the codebase.",
  "If a function accepts a sender argument, the caller's choice of argument determines which sending behavior is used. That choice is the enabling point. The call to sender.send inside the function is the place where the seam changes behavior without editing that call. Separating those two locations clarifies how the mechanism works. A test supplies a fake through the parameter; production supplies the real implementation through the same defined route.",
  "Consider code that calls a virtual method on a collaborator. A test can supply another implementation, changing what happens at that call without modifying the call itself. That is the seam idea. It lets tests avoid awkward infrastructure while still exercising the surrounding production logic. The important feature is selectable behavior, not a particular keyword or design pattern. Different languages offer different mechanisms, so inspect the actual binding and the place where it can be chosen.",
  "The enabling point selects which behavior reaches a seam. For object substitution, it may be a constructor argument; for another mechanism, it may be build configuration or linking. A test must control that selection deliberately. Otherwise it can accidentally exercise the real dependency despite the existence of an alternate implementation. Tracing from the enabling point to the behavior under test helps verify that the intended substitute is actually being used.",
  "A constructor that accepts a gateway can create the subject with a fake gateway for tests. The subject's later calls go through that object, so the fake controls the collaborator's behavior without replacing the subject's own logic. This is an object seam. The fake can return a known response and record requests for assertions. Keep a real gateway supplied in production and test that integration separately; the seam enables focused tests rather than making external behavior irrelevant.",
  "Supplying a behavior through a function argument makes the call boundary the place where a test can choose it. For example, a load function can receive a client rather than construct a fixed one. The name parameter seam describes how the choice is exposed, although the underlying mechanism may be object substitution or a callable value. What matters is that the test controls the argument and the production function actually uses it for the relevant operation.",
  "A useful seam gives the test control over a dependency that otherwise makes a case difficult. An injected random source can produce a chosen value so a rare branch is tested directly. A seam unrelated to the behavior being changed may not help at all. Begin with the test case and identify what needs control or observation, then choose the smallest suitable substitution point. This keeps testability work connected to a practical purpose.",
  "A function that directly uses a fixed global service gives its caller no obvious way to supply an alternative. A test may need to manipulate shared state or use language-specific replacement mechanisms, which can be fragile. Introducing an explicit collaborator can provide a clearer enabling point. First check for an existing seam and keep any preliminary change narrow. Global access does not mean substitution is impossible, but it often makes control less local and tests more likely to interfere.",
  "When introducing a seam, production should still select behavior equivalent to what it used before. Tests gain another selectable path, but ordinary users should not receive a different result simply because testability improved. For example, an injected clock defaults through explicit production setup to the real clock. Verify that wiring as well as the fake-based tests. A seam is meant to support safe change, so accidental changes in production selection undermine its purpose.",
  "The alternative implementation at a seam can be very small. A fake message sender may only record the last requested message because that is all the test needs to inspect. It does not have to reproduce mail delivery, retries, and networking. The simplicity makes the case easier to understand. Still honor the relevant contract: if the subject relies on a failure response, the fake must represent that case accurately enough for the assertion to mean something.",
  "An interface describes a possible contract, but code that always constructs one fixed implementation may remain difficult to test. The test needs a way to choose another implementation, such as a constructor argument or factory under its control. That selection route is what makes the abstraction usable as a seam. Before adding more interfaces, trace how the subject obtains its collaborators. A useful design gives the test both a compatible substitute and an enabling point through which to provide it.",
  "An environment variable can select alternate behavior, but it is usually shared by many tests in one process. If one test changes it while another runs, the second may unexpectedly use the wrong configuration. Restore changes carefully or prefer a more local enabling point when possible. The variable can still function as a seam selector; the concern is isolation and interference. A controllable dependency is most useful when each test can control it without disturbing others.",
  "A seam supports testing when the substitute behaves according to conditions the test chooses. A fake repository can return a missing record immediately, making an error path easy to exercise. The subject's logic still decides how to respond to that absence. If the substitute is itself unpredictable or requires the same infrastructure as the real dependency, much of the benefit disappears. Evaluate the seam by whether it enables a focused, repeatable observation of the target behavior.",
  "Checkout accepts gateway in its constructor and stores it. The constructor argument is the enabling point because that is where production or a test chooses the gateway implementation. Later calls through self.gateway use the selected behavior without changing Checkout's call sites. A test can supply a fake that records amounts or returns a chosen result. The snippet establishes the selection mechanism; the rest of Checkout must consistently use that collaborator for the seam to be effective.",
  "If the subject already calls a configurable collaborator, a test can change the supplied implementation without editing each place the collaborator is called. That is the advantage of an existing seam. For example, one injected clock can control all time reads that correctly go through it. Calls that bypass it and use the global clock remain outside the seam. Inspect the actual dependency path rather than assuming one configurable object automatically controls every related behavior.",
  "Adding a seam can itself introduce a mistake, so protect existing behavior where practical before changing how dependencies are selected. A characterization test or a broader existing test can provide a baseline. When no such test is feasible, use the smallest carefully reviewed dependency-breaking step and establish focused coverage as soon as possible. The recommendation is a safety direction, not a claim that legacy code always permits perfect tests before the first enabling change."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-maintenance/4");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
(() => {
  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-maintenance").sets.find(s => s.id === "6");
  const prose = [
  "Suppose a long invoice routine needs a new discount calculation, but changing its existing branches is risky. Put the discount rule in a separate method, test that method with representative orders, and call it from the old routine. This is a sprout method: new logic grows beside the existing implementation. The small connection still needs checking, since a correct discount calculation does no good if the invoice routine passes it the wrong amount.",
  "A new responsibility may need several operations and some state of its own. For example, a legacy checkout could delegate a new fraud assessment to a FraudChecker class. Tests can exercise that class without constructing the entire checkout system. Sprouting a class gives the responsibility a home, although the old caller still needs a small change to use it. Choose this boundary because the behavior belongs together, not merely to avoid editing any existing file.",
  "Imagine that every payment attempt must now produce an audit record. A wrapper can record the attempt, call the existing payment method, and then record its result. The old payment logic remains the implementation underneath. This is useful when the added behavior surrounds an operation rather than changing its internal calculation. Decide what should happen on exceptions too: code after the original call will not run automatically if that call throws.",
  "A sprout reduces the amount of legacy code you must disturb to add a feature. It does not untangle the original routine. If that routine already mixes billing, storage, and notification, calling one well-tested discount helper leaves those older responsibilities mixed together. Treat the sprout as a controlled way to introduce behavior, and retain a separate plan for cleanup when appropriate. A safe local addition and a coherent overall design are different accomplishments.",
  "A wrapper can reject invalid input before an existing operation or inspect its result afterward. For example, an authorization check can run before saving a document, while an audit entry can follow a successful save. Placement changes behavior: recording success before the save would be misleading if storage fails. Think through normal returns, exceptions, and rejected calls when deciding where the extra action belongs around the original method.",
  "Under a deadline, replacing a large untested routine can make the risk much larger than the requested feature. Isolate the new calculation in a small unit with explicit inputs and outputs, then make the smallest workable connection to the old path. You can test the new rule directly and check the connection separately. This contains the change; it does not guarantee that untouched code is correct or eliminate the need to verify affected behavior.",
  "Suppose an existing report method assembles rows, formats headings, and writes a file. A new rule for masking account numbers can become a separate method called during row preparation. Its tests can cover short numbers, missing values, and normal accounts without writing any files. That is the practical benefit of a sprout method: the new logic has a narrow testable boundary even though its caller remains difficult to test.",
  "A sprout class is useful when a new feature has a responsibility substantial enough to deserve its own object. A legacy importer might use a new AddressNormalizer that contains several related normalization rules. Keeping those rules together makes their tests and dependencies easier to understand. The class should accept the information it needs through a clear interface; if it reaches back into every part of the importer, the separation may exist only in the file layout.",
  "Wrapping preserves an existing operation as a callable unit and places extra work around it. A timing wrapper, for example, starts a timer, invokes the original method, and records elapsed time. A finally block may be necessary if failed calls must be timed too. The important boundary is the original call: the wrapper adds behavior at that boundary without copying and modifying the old method's internal statements.",
  "Untested legacy code makes a broad rewrite hard to evaluate because there is no dependable baseline for its many existing cases. A sprout lets you put a new requirement into code that can be tested from the start, while leaving most old statements alone. This is especially useful for a localized addition. If the requirement fundamentally changes the old algorithm, sprouting may only hide the necessary redesign behind a misleadingly small patch.",
  "A wrapper that is supposed to add logging must still perform the wrapped operation. Forgetting the delegation can produce convincing log messages while silently skipping the actual payment or save. Check that the wrapper calls the original method with the intended arguments and preserves its relevant return value and error behavior. Some wrappers deliberately block calls, such as authorization checks, but that exception must be part of the intended contract.",
  "A complicated discount rule might depend on membership, order size, and excluded products. Extracting it into a sprout method allows tests to vary those inputs directly, instead of navigating the entire checkout workflow for every case. This makes failures easier to interpret because each test targets the rule itself. Add a smaller number of integration checks to confirm that checkout supplies the right inputs and uses the returned discount correctly.",
  "One sprout can create a useful boundary; dozens of unrelated sprouts can leave a feature scattered across many helpers. A developer then has to reconstruct the feature by following calls through the old routine and its additions. Watch for repeated inputs, duplicated conditions, and helpers that always change together. Those patterns suggest that the accumulated behavior now needs a more deliberate responsibility boundary rather than another isolated patch.",
  "A wrap class can stand between a caller and an existing collaborator when both expose the interface the caller expects. For example, LoggingRepository implements the repository interface and delegates storage operations to a real repository after recording them. The caller can use the wrapper without learning a second calling convention. Compatibility also includes behavior: return values, exceptions, and ordering must remain appropriate for the contract being wrapped.",
  "Logging before delegation records that an operation was attempted. Logging after a successful return records that it completed. A wrapper is a natural place for these actions because it already controls the boundary around the existing call. Distinguish the two events in the log, particularly for operations that can fail. A line saying 'payment complete' before the payment method executes would create false evidence during incident investigation.",
  "Moving code into a new function does not automatically make it easy to test. If that function still reads global state, opens a database, and requires a running application server, its dependencies remain substantial. A useful sprout accepts the information or collaborators needed for its new rule through a manageable boundary. Testability comes from that controlled setup, not simply from giving a block of statements a different name.",
  "The connection point is where the legacy path begins using the new behavior. Keep that edit narrow enough to inspect confidently: one call with clear inputs is often easier to verify than several rearranged branches. Small is not sufficient by itself, however. The call must occur at the correct stage and its result must be used properly. A short integration test can catch a connection error that the sprout's own unit tests cannot.",
  "A precondition wrapper checks whether an operation is allowed before invoking the existing implementation. For example, it can reject a withdrawal amount that is negative. That changes what callers observe for rejected inputs, so the new condition needs an explicit requirement and tests. For accepted inputs, verify that delegation still occurs correctly. Wrapping offers a convenient location for the check; it does not make every new restriction compatible with existing callers.",
  "The original total calculation remains in legacy_total, while apply_new_discount owns the added rule. This lets tests exercise discounts using a known amount and order instead of reproducing all of the legacy calculation's setup. The surrounding function still matters: it must pass the calculated amount and return the discounted result. Test that connection as well, especially if the new rule depends on whether taxes or other adjustments have already been applied.",
  "A narrow sprout can be a reasonable deadline decision because it limits the code that changes. Verify the new rule and the affected old path so the shortcut does not become an unexamined production risk. Where a full regression harness is impractical, establish the most useful focused checks available and make the remaining uncertainty explicit. The technique trades broad redesign for a smaller change; it should not trade all verification for speed."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-maintenance/6");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
})();
