  const set = window.KOAN_COURSES.find(c => c.id === "software-maintenance").sets.find(s => s.id === "7");
  const prose = [
  "The visible edit is only one part of delivering a change. A developer might alter a condition in five minutes, then spend an hour rebuilding and another hour waiting for tests. The slow surrounding loop determines how quickly mistakes are discovered and corrected. Measure that whole path when evaluating change speed, rather than assuming that a small diff should automatically be cheap to release.",
  "When a build takes a long time, the developer cannot promptly learn whether the latest edit compiles and passes checks. By the time a failure appears, several other ideas may have been considered or changed, making diagnosis harder. Shortening the build creates a tighter connection between an action and its consequence. This is why build performance affects reasoning about changes, not merely the amount of time spent watching a progress bar.",
  "If every module depends on one widely included component, a small edit there may force a large rebuild. Separating responsibilities and reducing unnecessary dependencies can shrink that rebuild boundary. The improvement depends on the build system actually recognizing the new independence. Splitting files without changing the dependency graph may leave build time almost unchanged, so verify the effect rather than treating more modules as an automatic optimization.",
  "A focused harness creates enough of the system to exercise the behavior being edited, without starting every unrelated service. For a pricing rule, that might mean supplying a few order objects and a fake catalog. Tests can then run immediately after an edit and report a specific failure. Keep broader checks for integration, but use the focused harness to make the repeated edit-and-check cycle practical.",
  "A routine that validates input, computes prices, saves records, and sends messages makes a small change require understanding several concerns at once. Separating those responsibilities can narrow the context needed for a particular edit. The goal is not merely shorter methods: useful boundaries let a developer reason about one behavior while relying on clear contracts for the others. Poorly chosen splits can instead add navigation without reducing the mental work.",
  "Slow changes are not always caused by slow typing or difficult algorithms. A developer may spend most of the time arranging test data, waiting for a build, or getting a release through a manual process. Improving the surrounding tools and dependency structure can therefore matter more than simplifying the edited line. Start by finding where time is actually spent, then target the bottleneck that repeatedly delays feedback or delivery.",
  "The compile cycle is part of the feedback loop. If a syntax or type error takes fifteen minutes to appear, each small experiment becomes expensive, and developers may batch more edits together. Those batches make failures harder to trace back to one decision. Faster compilation supports smaller experiments and earlier correction. It improves the process most when compilation is a significant part of the actual delay.",
  "To estimate a change honestly, include learning what the code does, making the edit, building it, checking it, and getting it into use. A one-line fix can still be slow if its effects are difficult to understand or its release process is cumbersome. Separating these stages helps identify the real obstacle. Otherwise, a team may keep optimizing coding time while most of the delay occurs elsewhere.",
  "An edit that takes five minutes may require hours of verification because it affects a widely shared component. That verification is part of the cost of the change, even though the diff is tiny. Reducing the cost requires making affected behavior easier to isolate and check, or improving the relevant test pipeline. Simply urging developers to write the line faster cannot remove the surrounding uncertainty.",
  "A focused test target runs the checks relevant to a small area without rebuilding or starting every unrelated component. For example, testing a pure parser should not require compiling a user interface that never participates in parsing. This depends on real separation in the code and build graph. Keep a broader validation stage for cross-module effects; the focused target makes frequent local feedback affordable rather than proving the entire application correct.",
  "Coupling turns a local edit into work for other parts of the build. If many modules directly depend on an implementation detail that changes frequently, they may all need recompilation when it moves. A stable, narrower interface can reduce that exposure. The exact rebuild behavior depends on the language and tools, but the underlying question is consistent: which components must reconsider their assumptions when this component changes?",
  "When tests are expensive to run, developers tend to postpone them until several edits are complete. A failure then has more possible causes than it would after a single small change. This can erase the apparent time saved by running less often. A useful response is to create reliable fast checks for the inner loop while retaining broader checks at appropriate stages, so frequent feedback does not require the full suite every time.",
  "A hot spot is an area repeatedly involved in changes. A small characterization test can record an important existing result there before another edit is made. Because the test is fast and local, it becomes practical to run throughout the work. Choose behavior that matters to callers, not arbitrary internal details, and remember that one characterization case protects only the behavior it actually exercises.",
  "Before reorganizing a codebase to speed builds, measure where the build spends its time. The bottleneck might be dependency fan-out, a slow generator, unnecessary network work, or a serial step that dominates everything else. Each cause suggests a different fix. Measurement prevents an expensive architectural change that leaves the real delay intact, and a before-and-after comparison shows whether the chosen intervention helped.",
  "Extracting a module speeds testing when it removes unnecessary setup and dependencies from the behavior under test. A calculation moved behind an explicit input/output interface may no longer require a database or application server. Merely relocating the same tightly coupled code will not produce that benefit. Evaluate the extracted module by how independently it can be built and exercised, not just by whether it occupies a separate directory.",
  "A change is not available to users when the developer finishes typing; it is available after the release process succeeds. Slow packaging, approval queues, or deployment steps therefore contribute to total change latency. Measuring only local build time misses those delays. Improvements should preserve necessary checks while making the path more dependable and efficient, so faster editing translates into faster delivery rather than a larger waiting queue.",
  "A test that returns quickly but fails unpredictably can slow work by demanding repeated investigation. Useful feedback must arrive promptly and be trustworthy enough to guide the next decision. For example, a deterministic pricing test may be more valuable during an edit than a fast check against an unstable shared service. Improve speed and reliability together, and keep the scope of each check clear so passing results are not overinterpreted.",
  "When responsibilities are separated well, changing a tax calculation does not require understanding how email is formatted or how reports are stored. Clear inputs, outputs, and contracts establish what can be taken for granted across the boundary. This reduces the amount of code a developer must hold in mind. The benefit disappears if hidden shared state or undocumented side effects force the developer to inspect all the neighboring modules anyway.",
  "The command runs the pricing test module, which can provide quick feedback while pricing behavior is being edited. Its usefulness depends on those tests actually covering the affected cases and avoiding unnecessary setup. A passing result says nothing directly about tests that were not run. Use the targeted command repeatedly during development, then run the appropriate wider checks before treating the change as ready for release.",
  "Fast local checks help identify mistakes close to the moment they are introduced. Broader checks serve a different purpose: they exercise interactions that a small harness deliberately leaves out. A pricing unit test may pass while checkout supplies prices in the wrong currency. Keep both layers, using the narrow loop for frequent iteration and the wider checks at meaningful integration or release points."
];
  if (set.koans.length !== prose.length) throw new Error("Expound count mismatch: software-maintenance/7");
  prose.forEach((text, index) => { set.koans[index].expoundText = text; });
